Subject: register VM: fuse the chained property read

What this does:
  A get_field whose destination is immediately the receiver of the next
  get_field or get_field2 is lowered to a single instruction, ROP_get_field_chain.
  It performs both reads and writes both destinations, so it is exactly the pair
  it replaces: the first destination stays live and nothing observable happens
  between the two reads. What is saved is one dispatch and the second
  instruction's operand decode.

  Both PIC sites quicken independently. The generic handler tests both shapes
  inline; whichever read misses continues in a slow handler, and that handler
  rewrites the opcode byte -- to the _own spelling only when both sites are
  cached own slots, and back to the generic form otherwise, so the own handler
  may omit the kind and prototype tests. The first store cannot use T_SET_NEXT,
  because that dispatches and the second read has not run yet; the overwrite is
  inlined and the rare case where the old value's last reference dies continues
  in a separate free handler that releases it and then performs the second read.

  The fusion pass runs after register allocation and the ownership analysis and
  after dead-store elision, so neither sees the two-destination opcode. It fires
  only when the two instructions are IR-adjacent, the second's receiver operand
  is exactly the first's destination, the two destinations differ, and the
  second instruction is not a branch, catch or finally target and not a block
  leader. Site allocation was generalised to assign a dense PIC site to every
  's' operand rather than only the first.

Why we need it:
  On box2d get_field->get_field is 129.85 M of the dynamic instruction pairs,
  19% of all dispatches, and 67.36 M of those are chains of exactly this shape.

Adds:
  * quickjs-regvm.h: ROP_get_field_chain and ROP_get_field_chain_own, appended
    after the own read forms so no existing opcode number moves.
  * quickjs.c: the T_SZ_CHAIN "rrasrras" format, the four handler declarations
    and dispatch-table entries, the generic, own, first-miss, second-miss and
    first-free handlers, the two-destination site allocation, and the fusion
    pass with its adjacency and target checks.

Evidence:
  box2d fixed.js, clang-19 -O3, perf stat -r7: 29.165 G to 29.065 G
  instructions (-0.34%, -100 M), cycles -2.51%, wall -2.4%. On check.js the
  pass fuses 9,161,961 pairs (456,594 generic and 8,705,367 quickened),
  replacing 18,323,922 get_field-family dispatches; on fixed.js it fuses
  73,289,789 pairs, replacing 146,579,578 dispatches, which accounts exactly.
  A chain micro (40 M pairs) shows -3.0% instructions and -4.4% cycles. Box2d
  checksum 546.1008391043414 is unchanged and the register differential suite
  is 398/0/0 in both mono and threaded modes; a -DQJS_REGVM_VERIFY=1 -O1 build
  is clean and prints the same checksum.

  The measured saving is about 1.6 instructions per pair on box2d, not the 6
  the plan projected, because box2d's chained reads are mostly already-quickened
  own reads whose second half was already cheap.

Bytecode bump:
  No. The two opcodes are appended at the end of the enum so every existing
  opcode keeps its value, and neither is emitted by the encoder -- the generic
  form is produced only by the runtime fusion pass and the own form only by the
  quickening rewrite, so no blob contains them.

